    {
      "filename": "Portrait of James Craig by David Allan.jpg",
      "title": "File:Portrait of James Craig by David Allan.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a8/Portrait_of_James_Craig_by_David_Allan.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_James_Craig_by_David_Allan.jpg",
      "width": 897,
      "height": 1145,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "David Allan",
      "description": ":\nAugust, around what PUBLIC WORKS I see\nLo! Stately streets, Lo! Squares that court the breeze\nSee! Long canals and deepened rivers join\nEach part with each, and with the encircling Main\nThe whole enlivened Isle.\n(lines from James Thomson's poem 'Liberty' engraved on Craig's original plan for Edinburgh's New Town)\n\nCraig's original, imaginative but impractical conception was for the street plan to resemble the Union Flag with diagonal streets radiating from the centre. This was subsequently modified in favour of three parallel main streets running in a straight line between terminating squares. In the painting he is seen with a further modification which envisaged a circus as the central focus, but this was not adopted. The plan for a canal skirted by promenades in place of the drained valley of the Nor Loch was abandoned.",
      "dateCreated": "2013-03-12 18:23:03",
      "sourceType": "history",
      "sourceArticleId": "history-of-edinburgh",
      "sourceGeohash4": "gcvw",
      "smallPath": "_m/tpc/_ho/ed/in/bu/history-of-edinburgh/portrait-of-james-craig-by-dav-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Libberton Wynd, Edinburgh.jpg",
      "title": "File:Libberton Wynd, Edinburgh.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/Libberton_Wynd%2C_Edinburgh.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Libberton_Wynd,_Edinburgh.jpg",
      "width": 1181,
      "height": 1854,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "George Cattermole",
      "description": "\"Dowie's Tavern, Libberton's Wynd\" by George Cattermole. Libberton or Libberton's Wynd was a steep street which ran downhill from the Lawnmarket to the Cowgate, roughly on the line of the eastern side of present-day George IV Bridge. The wynd disappeared with the building of the bridge, which was completed in 1834. The innkeeper Johnnie Dowie kept a \"howff\" (favourite haunt) here which was frequented by Robert Burns during his stay in Edinburgh in 1786. Patrons included the painter Henry Raeburn, the poets Robert Fergusson and Thomas Campbell, the philosopher David Hume, the writer Christopher North, the song-collector David Herd and the brewer Archibald Younger. After Dowie's death in 1817, the new owner displayed a signboard with the name \"Burns' Tavern\".",
      "dateCreated": "circa 1854<div style=\"display: none;\">date QS:P571,+1854-00-00T00:00:00Z/9,P1480,Q5727902</div>",
